 # Record Store
 
-## Context
+## Context and Design Philosophy
 
 This node owns visit persistence and the meaning of a current visit when independently edited copies arrive. A visit is identified by a client-generated UUID and refers to a catalog plot ID. Updates can arrive days after their starting state was fetched. The server must not treat arrival order as evidence that one worker's observations are more authoritative.
 
-Tablet-sync and review-export are not yet designed. Their contracts depend on the concurrency mechanism selected here. Neither the HLD nor the catalog contract selects that mechanism.
+The design preserves each submitted visit as an intact unit. Coordinators resolve competing whole-visit revisions rather than field-level merges. This keeps submission metadata small and avoids rules that could combine scientifically related edits without review.
+
+Tablet-sync and review-export depend on this node's contract for submission envelopes, current-state representation, and unresolved-work signals. Both must link back to this node rather than invent their own concurrency semantics.
 
 ## Architecture
 
 The record store exposes authenticated submission, current-state retrieval, submission-history retrieval, and review-outcome operations. Persistence keeps original submissions, their authors, and server receipt times. A submission carries an idempotency key so a connection failure does not create another edit on retry.
 
-A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. Some edits are unrelated, such as correcting an observer name and adding a missing species. Other edits are related, such as revising a species identification and its cover estimate. The server cannot infer all such relationships from field names.
+A visit's scientific fields include observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, and notes. The server stores each submission as a complete visit revision and never discards original payloads.
 
-The selected concurrency policy will define the remaining submission metadata and review operations. They are deliberately not specified yet.
+The concurrency policy defines submission metadata and review operations. The chosen whole-visit revisions policy is described below.
 
 ## Submission and Retrieval
 
 Authenticate the submitting worker and validate field types and plot references before accepting a submission. Preserve the original payload separately from any later resolved or combined representation. A retry with the same key and identical payload returns the original acceptance result; reuse with a different payload is rejected.
 
-Retrieval must distinguish a usable current state from unresolved review work. Exactly how those states are represented remains part of the open choice.
+Retrieval distinguishes a usable current state from unresolved review work. A visit retrieval returns the resolved revision if one exists, plus a list of pending competing revisions. A visit with one or more pending revisions is considered unresolved for export.
+
+### Submission Envelope
+
+- `visitId`: client-generated UUID identifying the visit.
+- `baseRevisionId`: revision ID the edit was created from. `null` for the initial submission of a new visit.
+- `revisionId`: server-generated ID for this submission.
+- `idempotencyKey`: client-generated stable key for retries.
+- `authorId`: authenticated worker ID.
+- `receivedAt`: server timestamp.
+- `payload`: complete visit record with observation date, observer list, species cover estimates, notes, and plot reference.
+
+### Current-State Response
+
+- `visitId`
+- `resolvedRevisionId`: the revision currently selected for export, or `null` if no revision has been resolved.
+- `resolvedRevision`: the full payload of the resolved revision, or `null`.
+- `pendingRevisions`: array of pending revision summaries, each with `revisionId`, `baseRevisionId`, `authorId`, `receivedAt`, and `payload`. Presence of any pending revision marks the visit as unresolved.
+
+### Resolution Operation
+
+A coordinator resolves a visit by selecting one pending revision to become the new resolved revision. The operation records:
+- `visitId`
+- `chosenRevisionId`
+- `coordinatorId`
+- `resolvedAt`
+
+The chosen revision becomes the resolved revision. Other pending revisions remain stored for audit but are no longer pending. The system retains coordinator identity and contributing submission IDs with the resolution outcome.
+
+## Downstream Implications
+
+### Tablet-sync
+Tablet-sync must retain the base revision ID for each local draft and send it with every submission. On retrieval, tablet-sync must surface pending competing revisions to the worker so they understand their submission is under review. Local storage must accommodate the submission envelope described above and a retry state machine keyed by idempotencyKey. The tablet UI will need to explain that a visit is unresolved and that a coordinator review is pending.
+
+### Review-export
+Review-export must present a revision comparison screen that shows two or more whole-visit revisions side by side, with author, received time, base revision, and full payload diff. Export eligibility is governed by the unresolved-work signal: any visit with pendingRevisions must be held out of the partner CSV. The Friday export will include only visits with a resolvedRevision and no pending revisions. Review-export must also record coordinator identity and resolution time with the chosen revision.
+
+The field-aware three-way merge alternative remains credible because it would reduce review volume by automatically combining disjoint field edits and get more visits ready for export sooner. It would require tablet-sync to retain base snapshots and send structured field-level changes, and review-export to maintain field-level provenance and review only overlapping changes. The alternative was rejected because it requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and automatic combination can cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. The team prefers a larger review queue to avoid silent merging of scientifically related edits.
 
-## Open Design Choice: Concurrent Visit Edits
+## Decisions & Alternatives
 
-OPEN: select the concurrency policy before completing this node's public contract.
+| Decision | Chosen | Alternatives Considered | Rationale |
+|----------|--------|------------------------|-----------|
+| Concurrent edit policy | Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its base revision; competing complete revisions await coordinator resolution | Field-aware three-way merge with automatic combination of disjoint field changes and review for overlaps | Whole-visit preserves submitted visit as an intact unit, avoids merge rules that could combine scientifically related edits without noticing, and matches user preference for a larger review queue over maintaining merge rules. Review work is visible and export eligibility is clear. Field-aware merge was credible because it would reduce review volume, get more visits ready for export, and reduce coordinator workload. It was not chosen because it requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries, and automatic combination can cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. |
 
-Two approaches remain credible:
+## Open Questions & Future Decisions
 
-- Whole-visit revisions: each edit names its starting revision. Concurrent edits produce competing complete revisions for a coordinator to resolve. This preserves the worker's submitted visit as a unit and needs relatively little merge logic, but even edits to unrelated fields create review work. Tablet-sync would retain base revision IDs and display pending competing revisions. Review-export would need a revision comparison workflow and exclude unresolved visits from its export set.
-- Field-aware three-way merge: each edit supplies its base state and changes. Disjoint field changes combine automatically; overlapping changes require review. This reduces review volume and gets more visits ready for export, but requires maintained rules for scalar fields, observer lists, and species entries. Automatically combined fields can also cross scientific relationships the rules do not understand. Tablet-sync would retain base snapshots and send structured changes. Review-export would need field-level provenance and a review view for overlapping changes.
+### Resolved
+1. ✅ Concurrency policy selected as whole-visit revisions.
 
-The choice trades review workload and submission size against implementation burden and preservation of whole-visit intent. Neither approach may discard original submissions. Field-aware merging is not ruled out by a requirement that every combined record receive manual approval.
+### Deferred
+1. Whether to auto-resolve a pending revision when it is the only pending revision and its base matches the current resolved revision. Requires coordinator workflow design.
+2. Retention policy for superseded pending revisions after resolution.
 
